## How to write a board

Every board is a whole document that must render on its own. Start from this shape:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<title>What this board is</title>
		<meta name="board" content='{"w":1600,"h":1000,"bg":"grid"}' />
		<link rel="stylesheet" href="../lib/board.css" />
	</head>
	<body class="board">
		<section class="card" data-id="goal" style="left: 48px; top: 48px; width: 380px">
			<h3>Goal</h3>
			<p>One sentence that would survive being read out loud.</p>
		</section>
		<script src="../lib/board.js"></script>
	</body>
</html>
```

Four rules, and they matter more than they look:

1. **Position with inline `left`/`top`/`width` in `style`.** That is the one place you
   and the user can both edit — they drag a box, and the drag rewrites exactly that
   attribute. Use multiples of 8px.
2. **Give every component a `data-id`.** It is how you edit precisely later, how the
   canvas points the user at one box, and how they comment on it. Ids are stable
   names, not numbers: `goal`, `risk-refresh`, `next-steps`.
3. **Size the board in `<meta name="board">`** to fit what you put on it. Content does
   not scroll — the canvas is the scrolling — so a board that is too small clips.
4. **Two files in the head, always.** `board.css` and `board.js`; `board.js` loads
   markdown, maths, diagrams and PDF rendering itself, when a component needs them.

The components are documented in the **board-authoring** skill: read it before you
build a board with anything beyond cards and text. In short: `.card`, `.panel`,
`.sticky`, `.text`, `.chip`, `.callout` (`data-tone`), `.kpi`, `.table`, `svg.link`
(connectors between two `data-id`s), `[data-md]` (markdown + KaTeX),
`[data-mermaid]`, and `[data-embed]` for showing a real file.

## Showing the user's own documents

`[data-embed]` renders a file where it sits: markdown, PDF (with `data-pages="3-5"`),
HTML, or an image. Paths are relative to the board, exactly as in an `<img src>`.

```html
<div class="embed" data-id="paper" data-embed="../papers/oauth.pdf" data-pages="1-2"
     style="left: 600px; top: 140px; width: 460px; height: 680px"></div>
```

Inside the deck, any path works. Outside it, the file must be under a root declared
in `deck.json` — a path that is not renders as a card saying so, which means a
missing root is visible rather than silent.

